WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.383 --> 00:00:01.324
- Experience.

00:00:01.324 --> 00:00:04.170
Facing and overcoming
adversity is actually

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.743
one of your biggest advantages.

00:00:06.743 --> 00:00:10.017
You got to be open to taking some risks

00:00:10.017 --> 00:00:12.308
and not being afraid to fail.

00:00:12.308 --> 00:00:14.903
I want you all to stay
true to the most real,

00:00:14.903 --> 00:00:19.445
most sincere, most authentic
parts of yourselves.

00:00:19.445 --> 00:00:21.223
- She's an American lawyer, writer,

00:00:21.223 --> 00:00:23.853
and first lady of the
United States of America.

00:00:23.853 --> 00:00:26.663
She is married to the 44th
President of the United States,

00:00:26.663 --> 00:00:30.949
Barack Obama, and was the first
African American First Lady.

00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:33.688
She has become a fashion
icon, role model for women,

00:00:33.688 --> 00:00:35.786
and advocate of poverty awareness.

00:00:35.786 --> 00:00:36.944
She's Michelle Obama

00:00:36.944 --> 00:00:39.966
and here's my take on her
top 10 rules for success.

00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:41.581
As usual, as you watch if
there's something that she says

00:00:41.581 --> 00:00:43.202
that you really, really love

00:00:43.202 --> 00:00:44.424
leave it in the comments below,

00:00:44.424 --> 00:00:45.548
put quotes around it as well

00:00:45.548 --> 00:00:49.298
so that other people
can be inspired as well.

00:00:51.605 --> 00:00:54.279
- Some of you have been homeless.

00:00:54.279 --> 00:00:57.816
Some of you have risked the
rejection of your families

00:00:57.816 --> 00:00:59.797
to pursue your education.

00:00:59.797 --> 00:01:01.714
Many of you have lain awake at night

00:01:01.714 --> 00:01:03.779
wondering how on earth you were going to

00:01:03.779 --> 00:01:05.877
support your parents and your kids

00:01:05.877 --> 00:01:07.710
and still pay tuition.

00:01:08.655 --> 00:01:10.306
And many of you know what it's like

00:01:10.306 --> 00:01:12.277
to live not just month to month

00:01:12.277 --> 00:01:14.944
or day to day, but meal to meal.

00:01:17.326 --> 00:01:21.326
But graduates, let me
tell you you should never,

00:01:22.836 --> 00:01:26.393
ever be embarrassed by those struggles.

00:01:26.393 --> 00:01:30.624
You should never view your
challenges as a disadvantage.

00:01:30.624 --> 00:01:32.920
Instead, it's important
for you to understand

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.631
that your experience facing
and overcoming adversity

00:01:36.631 --> 00:01:40.436
is actually one of your biggest advantages

00:01:40.436 --> 00:01:43.282
and I know that because
I've seen it myself

00:01:43.282 --> 00:01:46.837
not just as a student
working my way through school

00:01:46.837 --> 00:01:47.992
but years later when I becam--

00:01:47.992 --> 00:01:49.150
After I ca--

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.518
Before I came to the White House

00:01:50.518 --> 00:01:52.498
and I worked as a dean in a college.

00:01:52.498 --> 00:01:54.256
In that role, I encountered students

00:01:54.256 --> 00:01:56.765
who had every advantage.

00:01:56.765 --> 00:01:58.717
Their parents paid their full tuition.

00:01:58.717 --> 00:02:00.854
They lived in beautiful campus dorms.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:04.146
They had every material possession
a college kid could want.

00:02:04.146 --> 00:02:07.026
Cars, computers, spending money.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:10.830
But when some of them got
their first bad grade,

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:12.431
they just fell apart.

00:02:12.431 --> 00:02:16.235
They lost it because
they were ill equipped

00:02:16.235 --> 00:02:20.731
to handle their first
encounter with disappointment

00:02:20.731 --> 00:02:22.148
or falling short.

00:02:24.116 --> 00:02:26.384
But graduates, as you all know,

00:02:26.384 --> 00:02:30.221
life will put many obstacles in your path

00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:33.302
that are far worse than a bad grade.

00:02:33.302 --> 00:02:36.230
You'll have unreasonable bosses

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.363
and difficult clients and patients.

00:02:38.363 --> 00:02:40.982
You'll experience illnesses and losses,

00:02:40.982 --> 00:02:43.552
crises and setbacks that
will come out of nowhere

00:02:43.552 --> 00:02:45.548
and knock you off your feet,

00:02:45.548 --> 00:02:48.283
but unlike so many other young people,

00:02:48.283 --> 00:02:50.282
you have already developed the resilience

00:02:50.282 --> 00:02:53.440
and the maturity that you
need to pick yourself up

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.607
and dust yourself off and
keep moving through the pain.

00:02:58.582 --> 00:03:00.395
(cheering)
Keep moving forward.

00:03:00.395 --> 00:03:04.234
Next, I've always felt a
deep sense of obligation

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:06.491
to make the biggest impact possible

00:03:06.491 --> 00:03:08.893
with this incredible platform,

00:03:08.893 --> 00:03:12.180
so I took on issues that
were personal to me.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:16.218
Issues like helping families
raise healthier kids,

00:03:16.218 --> 00:03:18.244
honoring the incredible military families

00:03:18.244 --> 00:03:20.413
that I've met out on the campaign trail,

00:03:20.413 --> 00:03:23.593
inspiring our young people
to value their education

00:03:23.593 --> 00:03:25.284
and finish college.

00:03:25.284 --> 00:03:27.113
(cheering)

00:03:27.113 --> 00:03:30.100
Now, some folks criticize my choices

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.913
for not being bold enough,

00:03:31.913 --> 00:03:34.996
but these were my choices, my issues,

00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:37.373
and I decided to tackle them

00:03:37.373 --> 00:03:40.091
in a way that felt most authentic to me,

00:03:40.091 --> 00:03:42.402
in a way that was both substantive

00:03:42.402 --> 00:03:46.846
and strategic, but also
fun and hopefully inspiring

00:03:46.846 --> 00:03:49.604
so I immersed myself
in the policy details.

00:03:49.604 --> 00:03:51.487
I worked with Congress on legislation,

00:03:51.487 --> 00:03:54.475
gave speeches to CEO's, military generals,

00:03:54.475 --> 00:03:55.755
Hollywood executives,

00:03:55.755 --> 00:03:58.117
but I also worked to
ensure that my efforts

00:03:58.117 --> 00:04:00.923
would resonate with kids and families

00:04:00.923 --> 00:04:03.380
and that meant doing thing in a creative

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.086
and unconventional way.

00:04:05.086 --> 00:04:06.454
So yeah, I planted a garden

00:04:06.454 --> 00:04:09.763
and hula hooped on the
White House lawn with kids.

00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:12.038
I did some mom dancing on TV.

00:04:12.038 --> 00:04:15.204
I celebrated military
kids with Kermit the Frog.

00:04:15.204 --> 00:04:17.089
I asked folks across the country

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:21.120
to wear their alma mater's
t-shirts for college signing day.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.287
And at the end of the day,

00:04:24.428 --> 00:04:28.499
by staying true to the
me I've always known

00:04:28.499 --> 00:04:32.855
I found that this journey
has been incredibly freeing

00:04:32.855 --> 00:04:35.060
because no matter what happened,

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:36.929
I had the peace of mind of knowing

00:04:36.929 --> 00:04:39.809
that all of the chatter, the name calling,

00:04:39.809 --> 00:04:42.902
the doubting, all of it was just noise.

00:04:42.902 --> 00:04:45.567
(cheering)
It did not define me.

00:04:45.567 --> 00:04:47.817
It didn't change who I was.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.340
And most importantly, it
couldn't hold me back.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.901
(cheering)
I have learned

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:57.175
that as long as I hold fast
to my beliefs and values

00:04:57.175 --> 00:04:59.273
and follow my own moral compass,

00:04:59.273 --> 00:05:03.440
than the only expectations I
need to live up to are my own.

00:05:05.409 --> 00:05:06.455
So graduates--

00:05:06.455 --> 00:05:09.675
You see, Barack and I were
both raised by families

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.595
who didn't have much in the way of money

00:05:11.595 --> 00:05:14.831
or material possessions, but
who had given us something

00:05:14.831 --> 00:05:16.331
far more valuable.

00:05:17.391 --> 00:05:20.215
Their unconditional love.

00:05:20.215 --> 00:05:22.348
Their unflinching sacrifice

00:05:22.348 --> 00:05:24.252
and the chance to go places

00:05:24.252 --> 00:05:27.502
they had never imagined for themselves.

00:05:28.425 --> 00:05:32.592
My father was a pump operator
at the city water plant

00:05:33.762 --> 00:05:35.468
and he was diagnosed
with multiple sclerosis

00:05:35.468 --> 00:05:38.262
when my brother and I were young

00:05:38.262 --> 00:05:41.797
and even as a kid, I knew
there were plenty of days

00:05:41.797 --> 00:05:45.246
when he was in pain and I knew
there were plenty of mornings

00:05:45.246 --> 00:05:50.117
when it was a struggle for
him to simply get out of bed,

00:05:50.117 --> 00:05:54.284
but every morning I watched my
father wake up with a smile,

00:05:55.948 --> 00:05:59.631
you know, grab his walker,
prop himself up against

00:05:59.631 --> 00:06:03.798
the bathroom sink and slowly
shave and button his uniform

00:06:05.143 --> 00:06:07.915
and when he returned home
after a long day's work,

00:06:07.915 --> 00:06:10.831
my brother and I would stand
at the top of the stairs

00:06:10.831 --> 00:06:15.133
of our little apartment
patiently waiting to greet him,

00:06:15.133 --> 00:06:17.424
watching as he reached
down to lift one leg

00:06:17.424 --> 00:06:21.975
and then the other to slowly
climb his way into our arms

00:06:21.975 --> 00:06:24.200
but despite these challenges,

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:28.340
my dad hardly ever missed a day of work.

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:29.995
He and my mom were determined to give me

00:06:29.995 --> 00:06:31.829
and my brother the kind of education

00:06:31.829 --> 00:06:34.665
they could only dream of.

00:06:34.665 --> 00:06:37.051
- What has been the
best advice you received

00:06:37.051 --> 00:06:39.382
from your teacher or professor?

00:06:39.382 --> 00:06:40.875
- Oh, I've gotten a lot of
great advice from teachers,

00:06:40.875 --> 00:06:43.415
mentors, professors throughout the years.

00:06:43.415 --> 00:06:47.575
So, first of all, you got to
be open to taking some risks

00:06:47.575 --> 00:06:50.064
and not being afraid to fail.

00:06:50.064 --> 00:06:51.908
Failure is the key to success

00:06:51.908 --> 00:06:54.671
so when you get to college,
you got to raise your hand.

00:06:54.671 --> 00:06:55.915
You have to be engaged.

00:06:55.915 --> 00:06:57.123
Don't be the kid thinking, well,

00:06:57.123 --> 00:06:59.700
I shouldn't be here and
maybe I'm not smart enough

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:01.477
and maybe, you know, no.

00:07:01.477 --> 00:07:05.018
You are more than capable
of going to college,

00:07:05.018 --> 00:07:06.806
being successful and going on,

00:07:06.806 --> 00:07:08.517
doing whatever you want in life.

00:07:08.517 --> 00:07:10.849
I sit here because I am you all.

00:07:10.849 --> 00:07:13.907
There is no difference between me and you.

00:07:13.907 --> 00:07:16.893
Working class kid growing up
on the South Side of Chicago,

00:07:16.893 --> 00:07:18.703
growing up with doubts

00:07:18.703 --> 00:07:20.571
and fears just like all of you all do.

00:07:20.571 --> 00:07:22.420
I know exactly how you all are feeling

00:07:22.420 --> 00:07:24.091
and kids out there who are thinking

00:07:24.091 --> 00:07:25.035
maybe I'm not ready.

00:07:25.035 --> 00:07:26.224
Maybe I'm not good enough.

00:07:26.224 --> 00:07:27.220
Maybe I can't afford it.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:29.515
But what teachers and mentors have told me

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:31.755
is that, yes you can and you must.

00:07:31.755 --> 00:07:36.144
So get in there and be bold
with your intelligence.

00:07:36.144 --> 00:07:37.302
Raise your hand.

00:07:37.302 --> 00:07:41.910
Get support when you need it
and know that we all got here

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:43.362
because somebody helped us.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:46.406
I want you all to stay
true to the most real,

00:07:46.406 --> 00:07:50.489
most sincere, most authentic
parts of yourselves.

00:07:51.419 --> 00:07:54.282
I want you to ask those basic questions.

00:07:54.282 --> 00:07:56.417
Who do you want to be?

00:07:56.417 --> 00:07:57.917
What inspires you?

00:07:58.977 --> 00:08:01.303
How do you want to give back?

00:08:01.303 --> 00:08:05.055
And then I want you to take a deep breath

00:08:05.055 --> 00:08:09.501
and trust yourselves to
chart your own course

00:08:09.501 --> 00:08:11.721
and make your mark on the world.

00:08:11.721 --> 00:08:14.814
Maybe it feels like you're
supposed to go to law school,

00:08:14.814 --> 00:08:18.853
but what you really want
to do is teach little kids.

00:08:18.853 --> 00:08:20.502
Maybe your parents are
expecting you to come back home

00:08:20.502 --> 00:08:21.920
after you graduate,

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:25.126
but you're feeling a
pull to travel the world.

00:08:25.126 --> 00:08:28.376
I want you to listen to those thoughts.

00:08:29.237 --> 00:08:34.091
I want you to act with both
your mind, but also your heart.

00:08:34.091 --> 00:08:36.651
And no matter what path you choose,

00:08:36.651 --> 00:08:40.577
I want you to make sure
it's you choosing it

00:08:40.577 --> 00:08:42.710
and not someone else.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:46.002
We learned about dignity and decency,

00:08:46.002 --> 00:08:48.684
that how hard you work matters more

00:08:48.684 --> 00:08:50.375
than how much you make,

00:08:50.375 --> 00:08:52.666
that helping others means more

00:08:52.666 --> 00:08:54.322
than just getting ahead yourself.

00:08:54.322 --> 00:08:57.178
(audience applauses)
We learned about honesty

00:08:57.178 --> 00:08:58.533
and integrity,

00:08:58.533 --> 00:09:00.828
that the truth matters,

00:09:00.828 --> 00:09:04.044
that you don't take shortcuts

00:09:04.044 --> 00:09:06.766
or play by your own set of rules.

00:09:06.766 --> 00:09:09.016
(cheering)

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:11.759
And success doesn't count

00:09:11.759 --> 00:09:14.944
unless you earn it fair and square.

00:09:14.944 --> 00:09:15.845
- Do you feel like,

00:09:15.845 --> 00:09:17.733
and I'm sure you did feel,

00:09:17.733 --> 00:09:21.877
pressure to be the first
African American First Lady to,

00:09:21.877 --> 00:09:25.284
there's some kind of responsibility or ...

00:09:25.284 --> 00:09:26.904
- Well, one of the things
I know from sitting

00:09:26.904 --> 00:09:29.075
in this position that every First Lady

00:09:29.075 --> 00:09:31.493
feels some level of pressure.

00:09:31.493 --> 00:09:32.900
I mean, this is a big platform

00:09:32.900 --> 00:09:34.124
and you don't want to mess it up.

00:09:34.124 --> 00:09:37.324
So I wouldn't dare to
compare my experience

00:09:37.324 --> 00:09:39.015
to any former First Lady,

00:09:39.015 --> 00:09:41.306
but I do take the role seriously.

00:09:41.306 --> 00:09:43.282
I do, as I said in my convention speech,

00:09:43.282 --> 00:09:44.861
I know that kids are watching us

00:09:44.861 --> 00:09:46.778
and watching what we say,

00:09:46.778 --> 00:09:48.844
what we do, and Barack and I have tried

00:09:48.844 --> 00:09:50.621
to make sure that what kids are seeing

00:09:50.621 --> 00:09:53.095
is something that they can be proud of

00:09:53.095 --> 00:09:54.533
because it matters.

00:09:54.533 --> 00:09:58.535
- You're getting advice from
around the world, it seems.

00:09:58.535 --> 00:10:00.420
Former British Prime Minister

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:02.162
Tony Blair's wife, Cherie Blair.

00:10:02.162 --> 00:10:04.331
Did you happen to hear
or read what she said?

00:10:04.331 --> 00:10:05.218
- No.

00:10:05.218 --> 00:10:07.348
- [Barbara] Well, she warns
that you will have to learn

00:10:07.348 --> 00:10:11.774
to take a back seat and
you will not be able

00:10:11.774 --> 00:10:14.129
to have any kind of a career

00:10:14.129 --> 00:10:18.296
and she says that that will be
a very difficult thing to do.

00:10:19.564 --> 00:10:22.459
- Yeah, I think it just
depends on the person.

00:10:22.459 --> 00:10:25.642
I have never been the kind of person

00:10:25.642 --> 00:10:28.950
who's defined myself by a career or a job.

00:10:28.950 --> 00:10:29.910
I just never have.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.368
People used to ask me that

00:10:31.368 --> 00:10:33.019
during the course of the campaign.

00:10:33.019 --> 00:10:35.565
Is it hard for you to step off the track

00:10:35.565 --> 00:10:38.398
and devote your life to his dream?

00:10:39.303 --> 00:10:41.863
But the truth is is that I believe

00:10:41.863 --> 00:10:44.459
in this man as our president

00:10:44.459 --> 00:10:46.003
and his vision for the country

00:10:46.003 --> 00:10:47.300
and if that meant, you know,

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:48.951
stepping away from my particular job

00:10:48.951 --> 00:10:51.673
for a year and a half or
for four or for eight years

00:10:51.673 --> 00:10:52.886
if you do what you're supposed to do,

00:10:52.886 --> 00:10:56.969
then, you know, that's a
small sacrifice to make.

00:10:58.691 --> 00:11:00.332
- And it's enough to keep you fulfilled?

00:11:00.332 --> 00:11:03.390
- Oh, well I've got these great kids too.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:05.275
I mean, and the truth is,

00:11:05.275 --> 00:11:06.987
and Barack knows this about me.

00:11:06.987 --> 00:11:09.487
I love kids and I love my kids

00:11:10.487 --> 00:11:13.473
and this isn't to say anyone
who's working doesn't,

00:11:13.473 --> 00:11:16.723
but I enjoy the balance of having a job

00:11:19.304 --> 00:11:20.793
and having a set of projects

00:11:20.793 --> 00:11:22.398
and being able to have times

00:11:22.398 --> 00:11:24.338
to make sure that I'm in the girls' lives

00:11:24.338 --> 00:11:27.106
and making sure that we
have time as a family.

00:11:27.106 --> 00:11:28.584
I mean, that's a full plate.

00:11:28.584 --> 00:11:31.876
I don't view myself as being in a position

00:11:31.876 --> 00:11:33.224
where I'm twiddling my thumbs

00:11:33.224 --> 00:11:36.603
and wondering how am I going
to get through the day.

00:11:36.603 --> 00:11:39.815
(chuckles) I've never
experienced that in my life.

00:11:39.815 --> 00:11:42.398
We are playing a long game here

00:11:43.309 --> 00:11:45.345
and that change is hard

00:11:45.345 --> 00:11:49.008
and change is slow and it
never happens all at once,

00:11:49.008 --> 00:11:51.443
but eventually we get there.

00:11:51.443 --> 00:11:53.025
We always do.

00:11:53.025 --> 00:11:56.314
We get there because of folks like my dad,

00:11:56.314 --> 00:11:57.896
folks like Barack's grandmother.

00:11:57.896 --> 00:11:59.801
Men and women who said to themselves,

00:11:59.801 --> 00:12:03.429
I may not have a chance
to fulfill my dreams,

00:12:03.429 --> 00:12:05.968
but maybe my children will.

00:12:05.968 --> 00:12:08.301
Maybe my grandchildren will.

00:12:09.173 --> 00:12:12.445
See, so many of us stand here tonight

00:12:12.445 --> 00:12:14.771
because of their sacrifice

00:12:14.771 --> 00:12:16.711
and longing and steadfast love

00:12:16.711 --> 00:12:20.265
because time and again
they swallowed their fears

00:12:20.265 --> 00:12:23.015
and doubts and did what was hard.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:24.903
♫ All the single ladies

00:12:24.903 --> 00:12:26.024
♫ All the single ladies

00:12:26.024 --> 00:12:28.421
♫ All the single ladies put your hands up

00:12:28.421 --> 00:12:30.009
♫ Up in the club,

00:12:30.009 --> 00:12:31.254
♫ Just broke up

00:12:31.254 --> 00:12:33.788
♫ Doing my own little thing

00:12:33.788 --> 00:12:34.880
♫ You decided to dip

00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:36.196
♫ And now you want to trip

00:12:36.196 --> 00:12:38.471
♫ 'Cause another brother noticed me

